 enjoy being active in  later life  and introduce them to activities and  sports through the can project and with  partner organizations  to provide opportunities for people to  embrace physical and mental health  and well-being and reduce isolation  through the various projects  programs and events that are delivered  the swim to the sea project started in  june 2019 and offered people age 55 and  over the opportunity to build skills and  confidence to enjoy  sea swimming the first six sessions were  pool based at timoth lido where the  participants trained for sea swimming  including swimming in wetsuits and  around boys and they built on their  confidence and fitness and skills  and the following six sessions were  based at timothe beach all sessions were  supported by pete wilby the swimming and  triathlon coach  trish warren the age uk devon active and  later life manager swimming coach  and can volunteers ambassadors and the  timmouth surf lifesaving club the  project was so popular  that a second project was set up  therefore they were known as waves one  and two as a result participants  increased their activity level  built on their open water safety  knowledge and swimming skills and made  great friendships and enjoyed social  meetings they continued to swim together  at the cannes winter wave sessions and  independently some of the groups are  even training to participate in their  first triathlon events and open water  events  organic art organic art is a small  charity running an arts and education  center  they work with creativity and land-based  learning to empower all people within  the community  encouraging a healthy and fulfilling  future  this year organic arts has worked with  nurseries local businesses  universities community groups ethnic  support groups  vulnerable adults teenagers facing  challenges  housing agencies people with learning  disabilities and more  they've engaged with over 4 000 people  in over 200 sessions  they planted vegetables made soup  pressed apples  woven willow explored the farm dug clay  made pots created sculptures ran  workshops  and held open days  [Music]  and the winner is andy mann's club  such an important message for supporting  men through suicidal thoughts and  encouraging us to talk thank you to all  our finalists and congratulations on  your achievements  the next category is always such an  impressive and inspirational moment in  the awards as we look to recognize the  lifetime  achievements of many of our local  residents who've devoted a huge amount  of energy  enthusiasm and volunteer hours to our  communities  the lifetime achievement award for sport  or community  is up next and here are the finalists  joan norman janet gorge  steve dinney sue hutchings  john campbell and here they are  joan norman joan is a veteran of the  voluntary sector  older than many of the people she helps  and is still very active in the  community having established a  befriending service  a coffee morning and helping to run the  weekly community lunch club having spent  her career helping others and being an  active member of her community in the  southeast of england she moved to  ashburton in 1970.  she started as part of the founding team  who established the volunteer driver  service in the town  attached to the local gp surgery whilst  volunteering as a driver for people  needing lifts to the surgery or hospital  she quickly spotted the need for  supporting people beyond their transport  requirements  social contact was also missing in their  lives and family relationships were  present were often not sufficiently  close to stop people feeling lonely  joan noticed this in 1995 when she first  volunteered with dash  in 1999 jones stepped in to develop a  visiting service and recruited others to  join her  eventually she would visit the ashburton  community hospital weekly to find out if  anyone there wanted a visitor or would  need support when they got back home  as well as ta