d grateful attitude at the beginning  of the pandemic  she started a fundraiser for homeless in  teen bridge support  raising over 4 500 pounds for the food  bank  at a time when it was needed more than  ever  steve dinney teachers come and go they  can all leave their mark on their impact  some more than others but steve dinney  has well and truly left his mark on  south dartmoor not only has he led teams  in both hockey and cricket to national  finals and not only has he encouraged  students to become elite performers but  he's also inspired them to participate  at all levels  inspiring them to believe in themselves  steve has been the beating heart of  south dartmouth for so many years  and he is that to ashmore hockey club  also it will be virtually impossible to  find someone as committed and successful  and dedicated as he is  steve has volunteered for sport all of  his life for way over 30 plus years and  has made an impact on thousands of  children and young people in all that  he's done  he always goes the extra mile and has  made a huge impact on the local  community  he's been the team manager for team  bridges hockey teams at the devon youth  games since the first ever one in 1999  and he's continued to guide and support  thousands of new volunteers  coaches players and officials in his  spare time he's truly extraordinary and  deserves to be recognised for a lifetime  of achievements both in education  and across the sporting community of  teambridge steve has our utmost respect  what can we say about someone who's  missed so much now that he's retired you  can't actually appreciate just how much  anything that anyone can write or say  about steve simply cannot do him justice  he's one in a million sue hutchings  the name sue hutchings is synonymous  with sport among the bovi tracy  community the south dartmoor school  sports partnership the coaching world of  uk lions hockey and the local hockey  community and the royal life saving  society at both local and national  levels  here at bobby tracy primary we feel that  a nomination is a fitting tribute to  everything that she brought to the  school in the 18 years she worked here  and indeed to all those children in the  wider local community that she's  inspired in the past and who she  continues to inspire today  when she retired last year in july 2019  we brought her a t-shirt emblazoned with  the word legend  to us that says it all sue developed and  ran numerous school clubs including  netball  football athletics running and sessions  for children identified as talented and  able to cover nutrition and sporting  ethos  she regularly qualified to take teams to  summer and winter devon school games  thanks to sue we won too many trophies  and tournaments to mention but more  importantly she encouraged children to  participate in  and continue with health sports and  fitness in her spare time sue volunteers  at bobby tracy lifesaving club and iska  hockey club  and she's currently the head hockey  coach for uk lionesses and west panthers  sue has spent her life inspiring others  through sport and physical activity from  a local level  to national standard john campbell  lifetime commitment to community sport  but especially for his 30 years plus as  a player  chairman manager groundsman linesman  referee ender season tour organizer lead  spokesman  general champion and charismatic and  genuine friend of everyone who's ever  been associated with sheldon villa fc  jon is singularly responsible for the  club existing at all and long championed  the cause for changing rooms through a  decade or more of pitch side football  strip donning he's seen generations of  players come and go  remaining the one constant figure  through untold turmoil including loss of  key personnel  both on and off the pitch on numerous  occasions jon's dedication to sheldon  villa has meant an  important community hub has thrived for  all these years providing a form of  sanctuary or diversion from the humdrum  and sometimes much worse for the legions  of players who've been drawn to the  convivial and inclusive ethos and  environment engendered by john there are  a multitude of men  in timoth sheldon and beyond who owe  lifelong friendships to the commitment  of john  turning 70 last year his surprise party  was an emotional affair with hundreds in  attendance to pay their respects  attendees raged in age from 5 to 80 male  and female demonstrating the draw of the  man and cementing his status as a  community giant and genuine friend to so  many  all in all john has had a phenomenally  interesting life enriched by his  decades-long commitment to sport  in team bridge which in turn is  something which has positively  influenced so much and so many  in the area  [Music]  and the winner is janet gorge  sadly janet lost the battle of a  terminal illness during lockdown  and we're sending all our thoughts and  best wishes to everyone that knew her  and all that she did for the community  the final award of the night goes to a  very special person who  touched the hearts of the community of  king stanton  there are no finalists this was a  nomination that stood out to the judges  and was given his own title of the  bernard jones  lifetime award let's hear more about  what bernard achieved  bernard jones was the former youth  chairman of king stanton afc  and president of pioneer league on the  9th of december 2019  he sadly passed away after dedicating  his entire life to the local community  and king stanton afc  the impact he had on the local community  was evident across the whole of king  stanton when the news of his passing was  released bernard's involvement with  youth football started in the 2007-2008  season he got more involved and  eventually took on the role as chairman  there were two teams under 11s and under  sevens it was in the 2011 season when  the club amalgamated with the rams and  became king stanton athletic youth  football club  from t